{-
   A global whose value the compiler supplies - `compiler/intrinsic/supplied.def`.

   The one direction of the coupling that was already checked before this framework existed, and the
   failure it was built for: `commandLineCount` used to be a name spelled in two code generators,
   matching a `pub let` that nothing verified, so renaming the library's global compiled cleanly and
   produced a program whose arguments were silently zero.

   `wordBits` is the other kind - the compiler decides what it holds. The initializer has to be zero,
   because a written number the compiler overwrites is a line of source that lies to whoever reads
   it, and the target's answer appears nowhere in the file. What lands there is an ordinary immutable
   global with a constant initializer, so it folds into its readers and costs a load nowhere - which
   is what the answer below demonstrates.

   The type has to be `Size`, which is the target's word rather than an integer that happens to be
   as wide as one here - see `BuiltinShape::Word` in `intrinsic/link.cpp`, which is where the
   difference is argued.

   `lib/Core/Bits.yana` is what claims the role, and it is claimed *once* - a second
   `@builtin(wordBits)` in this file is refused, because the compiler would otherwise reach whichever
   was read last. So what the fixture does is read the library's, which is the whole of what a
   program ever does with a supplied global. The shape rules a claim has to satisfy have no fixture
   any more - `Reject.Roles.yana` says why.
-}

fn main() -> Size = wordBits
